WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.960
<v Nora>Welcome back to Long Horizon Notes. Today is a debate — one question, two people who genuinely disagree. I'm joined by Daniel Cho, who runs a low-cost index practice, and Scott Bessent, a hedge fund manager who's built his career on concentrated bets. Welcome, both.</v>

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.780
<v Daniel Cho>Thanks, Nora. Happy to be the boring one today.</v>

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.240
<v Scott Bessent>And I'll be the reckless one, apparently.</v>

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:28.740
<v Nora>Before we dig in — today's episode is sponsored by Vanguard. Vanguard pioneered low-cost index funds. Try vanguard.com today.</v>

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:37.980
<v Nora>Here's the proposition: for an ordinary investor, is diversification the answer — or a way to hide from decisions you should be making? Daniel, you first.</v>

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:52.320
<v Daniel Cho>For an individual investor, diversification is the closest thing to a free lunch that exists. You give up almost nothing, and you remove the single biggest way people ruin themselves — one position going to zero at the worst possible time.</v>

00:00:52.320 --> 00:01:05.040
<v Daniel Cho>And I want to be precise: risk management for individuals is mostly behavioral. The math is easy. Sitting still while a holding gets cut in half is the hard part — and diversification is what makes it survivable.</v>

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:18.480
<v Scott Bessent>This is where I get off the bus. Concentration with deep understanding beats diversification. Spread across a hundred names and you own things you can't actually judge. That isn't safety — it's ignorance, evenly distributed.</v>

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:35.280
<v Scott Bessent>The way I see it, a portfolio is a system of correlated bets. A handful you understand deeply is more robust than a hundred you don't, because you can see how they move together. Diversifying without understanding just hides the correlation until it's the only thing that matters.</v>

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:44.940
<v Nora>Let me sharpen it, because the disagreement is real. Daniel, Scott says your safety is an illusion. Scott, Daniel says your edge won't survive your own behavior.</v>

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:59.160
<v Daniel Cho>That's exactly my claim. Diversification is the only real risk control — concentration is how retail investors blow themselves up. When we looked at the account data, the concentrated retail accounts had far fatter tails. We measured it.</v>

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:15.660
<v Scott Bessent>And I'd say the record shows the opposite for anyone who does the work. The honest question isn't diversify-or-not — it's whether your edge survives contact with your own behavior. If it does, concentration compounds it. If it doesn't, no amount of diversification saves you.</v>

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:19.020
<v Nora>We'll be right back after a quick word from our sponsor.</v>

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:25.620
<v Nora>Bloomberg Terminal is the data spine institutional desks run on. Visit bloomberg.com/podcast for a free trial.</v>

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:29.040
<v Nora>Welcome back. Is there a version where you're both right?</v>

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:39.900
<v Daniel Cho>Sure — Scott is right for Scott. For the professional who lives inside these systems, concentration is rational. My worry is when that advice leaks to people who can't do that work.</v>

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:51.540
<v Scott Bessent>And I'll give ground there. If you can't tell me why you own something, you shouldn't own much of it. Maybe the rule is: concentrate as much as your understanding allows, and diversify the rest.</v>

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:56.400
<v Nora>That might be the most agreement we get. One idea listeners can act on this week?</v>

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:03.120
<v Daniel Cho>For every position, finish the sentence 'I own this because…'. Anything you can't finish is a candidate to trim.</v>

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:09.600
<v Scott Bessent>Same exercise, opposite conclusion — anything you can finish with real conviction is a candidate to size up.</v>

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:18.000
<v Nora>And finally, a big thank you to our partners at Wealthfront. Wealthfront automates the boring parts of investing. Check out wealthfront.com.</v>

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.580
<v Nora>Daniel Cho, Scott Bessent — thank you both.</v>

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.080
<v Daniel Cho>Thanks, Nora.</v>

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.580
<v Scott Bessent>Good to be here.</v>

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.840
<v Nora>That's it for today's episode of Long Horizon Notes. See you next time.</v>
